[S] YASAP: Violet-Nec-Vam (Poly-Wish-Geno)

After ascending the Rog-Lyc I went back to my mission to ascend a
Necromancer Vampire.  Needless to say this was near trivially easy by
comparison.

There are 3 problems you face when playing a Vampire character.  The most
important, and immediate, is hunger.  Your regeneration is not hungerless.
You can only suck on fresh corpses (I think you get something like 4 turns
until the blood coagulates).  Until the Valley you probably won't find any
portable food.  Potions of blood and vampire blood are rarer than potions
of Poly before you regularly hit vampires who tote around such items.
Two other items help.  1) a ring of slow digestion.  2) a medical kit.
The latter is used to draw blood into a vial creating a potion of vampire
blood at the cost of 1 level (a pretty hefty price early on).  But medical
kits are ultra rare.  As is a ring of slow digestion.  Poly + PolyControl
would also work.  As would purposely getting Lycantropy and going around
as a wolf most of the game (some solutions are worse than the cure).  So
most of the beginning you just end up praying and watching your time-out.

I compounded my problems by playing a Necromancer.  Unlike the Wizard the
Necro doesn't get hungerless spell casting.  So even though I started out
with Magic Missile I basically couldn't use it otherwise I'd screw up my
scheduled prayer time-out stomach filling.

The second problem doesn't affect you too much until the middle game.
Because Vampires suck their prey they only have a small percentage chance
of aquiring the usual intrinsics.  I think I sucked about 14 floating eyes
before getting ESP.  There are a few ways to solve this problem.  You can
aquire a tinning kit, ring of poly control and a way to poly (wand,
potion, trap).  The tinning kit is useful if you don't have a ring of poly
and need to store up your intrinsic food for one sitting.  Another way is
to get crowned.  But doing this too early when food is still a problem and
your sunk.  The third way is to get Lycantropy and use your wolf form
to eat.  The fourth way is to just suck prey and hope for the best.
I've used the poly trick before, but this time I just took the sucking
route.

The third problem doesn't happen until later in the game and ironically
it's the opposite of the first problem; you get full.  Being a vampire you
need to keep close watch on how full you stomach is.  If you're satiated
you'll eventually fill up and when you attack (and bite) prey you will get
messages like "you're finally finished".  And I believe it means you lose
a turn in combat.  It sucks.  So add to your ascention kit a ring of
Hunger.  Weird, no?


Violet started out with 3 good items: spellbook of MM (much better than
the useless Knock I usually get), a good constitution (14), and a ring of
poison resistance.  This last one really is quite a boon since getting the
intrinsic is so hard.  And i've lost more than one vamp to poison needles
and other fatal posions.  Not to mention it makes your menu a bit larger.
And this more than made up for the other starting ring being Regeneration.
Personally, I think this shouldn't be a legal item in a vamp's starting
inventory.

Starting with a ring of slow digestion is almost cheating since it
completely eliminates one of the problems unique to Vamps and Lyc.  This
should also be programmed around IMO.

On Dlvl 2 I found a shop that had a Lab Coat, so now both my rings were
redundant.  Oh well. Even so, I don't think I got the anti-poison
intrinsic until just before the quest.

I found a ring of slow digestion in Sokoban.  About 1000 turns later I
lost it to a trapped chest on Dlvl 4.  Put all my combustibles in a bag,
but forgot about the electrical charge.  Almost cried.  My game was going
so well.

I had a lot of luck with items and !ofGL.  Just before the quest I dipped
for a better cloak and got CoMR.  Then dipped for better gloves after the
quest and got GoD.  Then much later dipped for a better helm than my elf
hat, got HelmOfBrains.

Found lots of magic markers this game.  Discovered that confused +
?ofCharging == more Mana.

One of the bigger problems is finding a good weapon for the Necromancer.
The gift dagger is great until everyone you meet is poison resistant.  The
quest dagger is good until everyone you meet is a demon or chaotic.
Neither is good for below the Castle.  I charged up a red lightsaber to
+6.  But then it ran out of juice.  And as I posted, after charging it up
with a scroll it reverted to the +1 status I found it in (bug).  After
that I kept it for Demo and Astral.  Otherwise I used a +6 Unicorn horn
even though I was restricted in that skill.

Real trouble came in the Chaotic Quest.  The level generated with about 6
purple Ls.  A summoning storm quickly started.  A Fire Vampire threw a
potion of Amnesia at me (man, these are evil!).  I fought my way back to
the portal and escaped.  Then I went to an altar 2 lvls down and had a
sac-fest waiting to get Crowned (for 1 missing intrinsic: fire) and
waiting for a *trice to drop a corpse so I could clear out the Chaotic
Quest level.

The Necro Quest Nemesis is a demon, so he's a bit hard to deal with.  You
can't death ray him, your poison dagger doesn't do squat against him and
you can't magic missile him once he has the dagger.  So again, it's a
problem of effecient damage.  Killing him before he offs you with his
headache spells is rather difficult.

I made a pretty big mistake at One-Eyed-Sams.  I planned to take him out
and thus went around his shop racking up a bill.  I used some scrolls of
earth to seal him in and then planned to Magic Missile him to death.  Of
course, I forgot that he's got reflection.  Then I tried with a pole-arm
and ESP.  Nope.  Then I spent a magic marker writing Draining (which I'd
forgotten) since I thought I'd use that to kill him.  His MR is too high,
or he's drain resistant.  I couldn't tell and I was getting hungry.  So I
had to break Poly-Self conduct and Xorn out of there.

I tried a new trick to defeat Demo.  Of course, being sickness resistant
made him not much of a threat, but you can't get sloppy either.  One of my
promising Vamps on the Antisymmetric server died of headaches.  Well, this
time I dropped a scare monster on the stairs.  When he jumped me I
teleported him away (not sure if this is necessary) and drank a cursed
potion of gain level.  Then I came back down the stairs onto the scare
monster scroll.

All the rest was pretty rudimentary.

